---
// Tutor-set pricing deep dive — DRAFT. Served at /tutor-pricing in local preview only
// (see src/pages/[draft].astro). Spans marked <Tk> are prompts for details only Andrew has;
// they render as red dashed boxes so nothing unfinished can slip through unnoticed.
// To publish: fill every <Tk>, move this file to src/pages/tutor-pricing.astro, remove it
// from src/pages/[draft].astro, remove `noindex`, and drop `draft: true` in src/data/site.ts.
import CaseStudy from '../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Stats from '../components/Stats.astro';
import Tk from '../components/Tk.astro';
---

<CaseStudy
  name="Tutor-set pricing"
  lead="Tutor-set pricing:"
  tagline="Letting tutors set their own prices without a race to the bottom. 70% opted in within 3 months."
  noindex
  details={[
    { label: 'Responsibilities', items: ['Product design', 'User research', 'Product building'] },
    { label: 'Collaborators', items: ['1 product manager', 'TK engineers'] },
    { label: 'Platforms', items: ['Web'] },
    { label: 'Years', items: ['TK'] },
  ]}
>
  <Stats
    slot="meta"
    items={[
      { value: '70%', label: 'of tutors opted in within 3 months' },
      { value: 'TK', label: 'change in tutor take-home pay' },
      { value: 'TK', label: 'second outcome (e.g. bookings or revenue)' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="intro">
    <p>
      Part of <a class="text-link" href="/dojo-tutor">Dojo Tutor</a>, ClassDojo’s marketplace connecting tutors and
      families. Prices used to be fixed by the platform. We wanted tutors to set their own, so they could charge more
      for specialty topics and open up more affordable options for families. I led the design and research, and built
      much of the frontend.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure images={[{ name: 'dojo-class-prices', alt: 'Editing a class price with the recommended range and take-home pay' }]} eager />

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>The stakes</h2>
    <p>
      A fixed price treated every tutor as interchangeable. That capped what experienced and specialist tutors could
      earn, and priced out families who needed something more affordable. Moving pricing to tutors was the step from a
      matching service toward a true marketplace.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Tk>What was at risk for the business: e.g. platform revenue, tutor churn, family conversion. One or two
      sentences.</Tk>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>What made it hard: fear of a race to the bottom</h2>
    <p>
      Research showed that most tutors wanted to set their own prices, but many worried about a “race to the bottom” as
      tutors competed on price.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Tk>How you learned this: who you talked to, how many tutors, what methods (interviews, surveys, data).</Tk>
    </p>
    <p>
      <Tk>A quote or a specific finding that captures the fear.</Tk>
    </p>
    <p>
      The real problem wasn’t the price input. It was confidence: tutors needed to believe that pricing themselves would
      pay off, and that their value would be visible beyond a number.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>Options we weighed</h2>
    <p>
      <Tk>Option 1: what it was, and why you didn’t ship it.</Tk>
    </p>
    <p>
      <Tk>Option 2: what it was, and why you didn’t ship it.</Tk>
    </p>
    <p>
      <Tk>Where you disagreed with someone (PM, leadership, tutors) and how you resolved it.</Tk>
    </p>
    <p>
      We landed on opt-in, tutor-set pricing with guardrails: marketplace fees calculated to increase tutor take-home
      pay, recommended ranges based on market data, and tools that help tutors communicate their value instead of
      competing as an undifferentiated listing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>What we shipped</h2>
    <p>
      I designed and built much of the frontend for tutor pricing, including the first-time experience, setting rates,
      adding discounts, and surfacing market data so tutors can price competitively.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure
    images={[
      { name: 'dojo-pricing-earnings', alt: 'Estimated earnings increase from the new pricing model' },
      { name: 'dojo-pricing-setup', alt: 'Setting 1:1 tutoring prices during onboarding' },
    ]}
    lead="Opting in."
  >
    Tutors see what they stand to earn before switching, then set prices with recommendations based on similar
    classes. Leading with take-home pay answered the race-to-the-bottom fear before a tutor typed a number.
  </Figure>

  <Figure
    images={[{ name: 'dojo-pricing-modal', alt: 'Setting a group class price with Earnings Boost' }]}
    lead="Pricing with guardrails."
  >
    Market-based recommended ranges, take-home pay after fees, and the Earnings Boost for long-term family
    relationships. <Tk>Why the Earnings Boost exists and what it rewards.</Tk>
  </Figure>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      Despite widespread apprehension from our tutor community, over 70% of tutors opted into the new pricing model
      within the first 3 months of release.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Tk>What happened to prices: did they go up, down, spread out? Any sign of a race to the bottom?</Tk>
    </p>
    <p>
      <Tk>Impact on families and the business: bookings, revenue, take-home pay.</Tk>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>What I’d do differently</h2>
    <p>
      <Tk>One honest lesson: something you’d change, test earlier, or that surprised you.</Tk>
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="back">
    <a class="more" href="/dojo-tutor">‹ Back to Dojo Tutor</a>
  </p>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .back {
    margin-top: clamp(48px, 6vw, 80px);
  }
</style>
